{{smaller block|<poem>Alle his vesture uerayly watȝ clene ''verdure'',
Bothe the barres of his belt & other blythe stones,
That were richely rayled in his aray clene.</poem>
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 161.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Innepee she lepte the fenestre vppon,
Aboue beheld she ''uerdures'' flouresshing.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3823.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Plants of eternal ''verdure'' only grew
Upon that virgin soil.</poem>
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, ii. 196.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Bleak winter flies, new ''verdure'' clothes the plain.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', tr. of Milton’s Latin Elegies, v.}}}}
Hence —'''2.''' Freshness in general.
{{smaller block|Whatsoever I should write now, of any passages of these days, would lose the ''verdure'' before the letter came to you.
{{right|''Donne'', Letters, lix.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''decorative art'', tapestry of which foliage or leafage on a large scale, scenery with trees, or the like, is the chief subject. Also ''tapis de verdure.''
{{smaller block|A counterpaynt of ''verder''. . . . iije gret kerpettes for tables ii . . . of fyne arres and the other of ''verder''.
{{right|''Dame Agnes Hungerford’s Inventory'', temp. Henry VIII.}}
{{right|[(Archæologia, XXXVIII. 364).}}}}

'''verdure''' (vėr′d̮ūr), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''verdured'', ppr. ''verduring''. [< ''verdure'', ''n.''] To cover with or as with verdure: as, “''verdured'' bank,” ''Parnell.''
{{smaller block|One small circular island, profusely ''verdured'', reposed upon the bosom of the stream. {{right|''Poe'', Tales, I. 363.}}}}
